body:
@copilot This is good! I think we're nearly there! I'm concerned that the whole from the screw to the funnel at the bottom is too small. See the picture below for context; it's a screengrab of the auger in a slicer, on layer 13. The only gap from the screw to the funnel is that tiny ring. You can download and use a slicer to see for yourself if you feel you need to.

Try opening that up that gap a bit more. I still like the screw continuing down as the auger funnels, and a bit more of an opening would make it better. Be careful not to change anything else--this design is already good; we just need this small adjustment.

<img width="1129" height="864" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4c4e3915-4e6d-4dff-9dba-65affd20da9c" />

@copilot this is close! Shorten the length even more, chamfer the back corners (like in the drawing) and move the mounting holes to match the placements of the green holes in the drawing. Note that the drawing has a top, side, and front view. Check your views against the drawing when you're done, make sure they are consistent

Right now, the triangular supports are floating far behind the part. Move them up to be right underneath the servo mounts; they should be intersecting the flanges and the base plate. If you need to, you can put them in line with the front edge in between the hinges. But they have to be intersecting the flanges. The horizontal mounting hole should be on the support, not on the flange, i.e. it should be parallel to the auger at a 0 degree position. 

Also, we don't need the mounting plate to be that long. The front half is great (especially the mirrored gear--nicely done!), but move the back bracket up until it's just behind the servo mount and cut off the excess behind it--it should be a little more than half the previous length.

@copilot this is much better! The hardstop is perfect, especially with the mounting hole through it. Consider adding a countersink for the mounting screw so the hardstop top is flat.

The mounting plate still interferes with the collar. Make a space in the mounting plate for the collar, using the same tolerance as the brackets in #46. Thus the collar should be able to freely rotate in that space. There should be a collar-shaped cutout in the mounting plate; remove the intersecting space, with some clearance.

Additionally, the coin and solenoid mounting plates aren't reinforced the way I intended. I meant that directly at the intersection of the plates and the collar circle there is a weak point. You can fill in all that space, i.e. make it look like the mounting plates are coming out of the collar. Make it a rectangle, not a line and a circle. This is similar to the bracket design from #46 and #47, in which the intersection between the bracket and the mounting plate was reinforced. You can use that as a template.

This will absolutely not work. The solenoid axis runs parallel to the mounting plate (and to the plane of its mounting points), meaning that in the current design, the solenoid will run parallel to the current mounting plane and thus to the auger; in this configuration, it won't hit the auger--its axis is parallel to the auger, not perpendicular like it should be. This will likely require a new face and a potential widening of the tap collar. Re-check the diagram. 
Additionally, the tip of the solenoid when extended should be 3mm into the auger (interfering). That way, when extended, it will hit the auger with some force. 
@copilot using Opus 4.8, apply these changes and any relevant changes from your review
